course: technology
lesson: 43
title: Olympiad: transport, building and energy ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This last olympiad lesson takes you out of the house: onto runways and canals, through tunnels and over bridges, up to the satellites and into the power station, with puzzles that need two or three steps.
explain: Many puzzles hide a number in a name: runway 27 is really 270 degrees on the compass, so it points west, and the same strip seen from the other end is runway 09, pointing east.
explain: Picture a puzzle card at a club: «The space station goes round the Earth about every 90 minutes. How many times a day?» A day is 24 hours, which is 1,440 minutes, and 1,440 split into 90s makes 16.
explain: Some puzzles test what people say: «the tunnel's like a thousand miles long» is hyperbole for a long tunnel, and «bit nippy, roads are like glass» can mean real ice.
explain: Check the shape, the number and the reason, and the answer will follow.
fact: Runway numbers are the compass direction divided by ten: runway 27 points to about 270 degrees, which is west, and the other end of the same strip is runway 09, pointing east.
fact: The International Space Station goes round the Earth about every 90 minutes, which is about 16 times a day.
fact: Almost all electricity comes from something spinning a generator; solar panels are the exception, because in them light knocks tiny electrons free in silicon cells.
fact: In the UK, circular road signs give orders, triangular signs warn and rectangular signs give information.
fact: Gritters spread salt before a frost because salty water freezes at a lower temperature than plain water, so ice forms less easily.
fact: A bus can be about 12 metres long and a family car about 4 metres, so one bus is about as long as 3 cars.
fact: The Channel Tunnel is really three tunnels, two for trains and one service tunnel between them; it is about 50 km long and opened in 1994.
fact: The water that fills a lock is its length times its width times the height the water rises: a lock 20 metres long and 2 metres wide that rises 3 metres takes 120 cubic metres.
fact: In the UK all satellite TV dishes on a street face the same way, south-east, because they aim at satellites far above the equator.
fact: Bridges grow longer in heat and shorter in cold, and expansion joints, metal teeth or gaps, let them move.
fact: A hot-air balloon rises because hot air is lighter than the cold air outside; its pilot cannot steer left or right, only go up or down to find winds blowing other ways.
fact: A Mars rover drives itself short distances because radio signals take minutes to travel between Mars and Earth.
exercise: A plane takes off from runway 27. Which way is it heading, and what is the other end of that runway called? | West; the other end is 09 | East; the other end is also called 27 | West; the other end is called 72 | North; the other end is 18
exercise: The space station goes round the Earth about every 90 minutes. About how many times does it go round in a day? | About 16 | About 1 | About 90 | About 24
exercise: Odd one out: which does NOT make electricity by spinning a generator? | A solar panel | A wind turbine | A hydroelectric dam | A nuclear power station
exercise: Without reading the words, what do you know about a round road sign in the UK? | It gives an order | It warns of a danger ahead | It gives information like directions | It shows a place where cars may park
exercise: Why does salt on the road help stop ice? | Salty water freezes at a lower temperature than plain water | Salt is warm and heats the whole road up | Salt soaks up all the water so none is left | Salt makes car tyres sticky like glue
exercise: About how many 4-metre family cars, parked nose to tail, are as long as one 12-metre bus? | 3 | 4 | 8 | 16
exercise: The Channel Tunnel has three tunnels. How many of them carry trains? | 2 | 3 | 1 | 6
exercise: A lock is 30 metres long and 4 metres wide, and the water rises 2 metres. How many cubic metres of water flow in? | 240 | 36 | 120 | 2,400
exercise: Why do all the satellite dishes on a UK street point the same way? | They aim at satellites high above the equator | The wind has blown them all round | The council tells everyone which way | They point at the nearest phone mast on the hill
exercise: On a hot summer day, how long is a long steel bridge compared with a cold winter day? | A little longer | A little shorter | Exactly the same length | Twice as long
exercise: A balloon pilot wants to go east, but the wind near the ground blows west. What can she do? | Go up or down to find a wind blowing east | Steer east with a rudder like a boat's | Point the burner flame sideways to push | Wait on the ground for the wind to turn
exercise: Why can't a Mars rover be driven live with a joystick from Earth? | Signals take minutes to get there and back | Mars is too dark for its cameras to work at all | Joysticks freeze solid in the cold on Mars | Radio does not work in space
exercise: Which order is right in a power station? | Fuel heats water, steam spins the turbine, the turbine turns the generator | The generator heats water, steam burns fuel, the turbine spins the steam | Steam spins the generator, the generator burns fuel, fuel heats the turbine | Fuel turns the generator, the generator boils water, water burns the steam
exercise: Odd one out: which of these is NOT a reason the Channel Tunnel has a service tunnel? | So boats can sail through under the sea | So workers can reach the tunnels | So people have a safe way out in an emergency | So air can be kept fresh in the tunnels
exercise: «the Channel Tunnel's like a thousand miles long, took forever» About how long is it really? | About 50 km | About 1,000 miles | About 5 km | About 500 km
exercise: Two lighthouses stand on the same coast. How does a sailor at night know which one is which? | Each one flashes its own pattern | The nearer one is always brighter | One is red, the other always blue | The keepers shout their names across the water
test: ★★★ «gps works coz the satellite watches me from space and sees where i am, no cap» What is right? | No, the phone hears timing signals from several satellites and works it out | Yes, GPS satellites have giant cameras that follow every phone | Yes, but only when the sky is clear and sunny | No, GPS uses the phone masts on the hills, not satellites
test: ★★★ «the lorry's 12 m — nah hang on, with the trailer it's 16 m. how many 4 m cars long is that» | 4 | 3 | 7 | 64
test: ★★★ «saw the space station go round like a million times today lol» About how many times does it really go round in a day? | About 16 | A million, it is very fast | About 90 | Just once
test: ★★★ «we're landing on runway 09 so we're flying west innit» What is right? | No, 09 means about 90 degrees, so the plane is flying east | Yes, all runways with a 0 in front point west | No, 09 means it is the ninth runway at the airport | Yes, 09 means 9 o'clock, which is west
test: ★★★ «bit nippy out, roads are like glass, but the gritter's been» What is the speaker really saying? | It's freezing and icy; the salt helps, but take real care | It's a warm day and the roads are shiny and clean | The roads are made of glass and the gritter swept them | It's a little cool, so ice is impossible today
test: ★★★ «solar panels are just tiny wind turbines with a spinny bit inside» What is right? | No, light frees tiny electrons in silicon cells and nothing spins | Yes, a little fan inside spins when sunlight warms it | Yes, they need wind as well as sunlight to work | No, they burn sunlight like a small fire
test: ★★★ «there's a gap with metal teeth across the bridge road, someone forgot to finish it lol» What is it really? | An expansion joint, so the bridge can grow and shrink | A crack showing that the bridge is about to fall down | A drain for rain to run off the road into the river | A place where the bridge folds up for ships
test: ★★★ «a round sign with a red ring is just a warning, ignore it» What is right? | No, round signs give orders; triangles are the warnings | Yes, round signs are only suggestions for drivers | Yes, red means warning on every road sign | No, round signs show places to eat
test: ★★★ Which order is right for a tunnel-boring machine? | The wheel grinds the rock, the rock is carried away, concrete rings are fitted | Concrete rings are fitted, then the wheel grinds, then the rock is carried away | The rock is carried away, then the wheel grinds the hole, then rings are fitted | The rings are fitted first and the machine grinds the rock out through them
test: ★★★ «hot-air balloon floats coz it's full of helium» What is right? | No, a burner heats the air inside, and hot air is lighter than cold | Yes, it is just like a party balloon, only a lot bigger and filled from tanks | No, it is pulled up by a hidden rope | No, its basket is lighter than air
test: ★★★ A friend wants to race a Mars rover live with a joystick, «like a remote-control car, ez». What is the catch? | Each command takes minutes to reach Mars, so the rover drives itself | Mars has no roads, so the rover cannot move at all | Remote-control cars and rovers use different batteries | The rover is too heavy for a joystick
exam: It is given that an aircraft departs from runway 27. Determine the compass direction in which it is heading. | West | East | North | South
exam: The International Space Station completes one orbit of the Earth in approximately 90 minutes. Ascertain the approximate number of orbits completed in one day. | Sixteen | Ninety | Twenty-four | One
exam: Identify, with reference to the established facts, the one source of electricity which requires no spinning generator. | The solar panel | The wind turbine | The hydroelectric dam | The coal-fired power station
exam: A canal lock measures 30 metres in length and 4 metres in width, and its water rises by 2 metres. Determine the volume of water admitted, in cubic metres. | 240 | 120 | 36 | 2,400
